 {"id": "AUG-058", "title": "option.costs and option.breaks are declared columns no verb can ever set: dump prints costs=\u2014 breaks=\u2014 forever", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 1, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run)\n\nThe `option` table declares `costs` (varchar) and `breaks` (varchar) columns\n(auger.py COLS). `answer` inserts every option row with those fields as EMPTY\nSTRINGS and no verb anywhere in the CLI sets them:\n\n  grep for an add_argument covering costs/breaks across the whole\n  subparser registry -> zero hits.\n\nMeanwhile `dump` renders them for every option:\n\n  [ ] D-003-O1  <label>  costs=\u2014  breaks=\u2014\n\nSo the rendered configuration carries two fields that are structurally always\nempty. That is worse than omitting them: a reader of the dump sees \"costs=\u2014\" and\ncannot tell whether the option has no cost or auger simply cannot record one.\n\nThis is the option-level analogue of AUG-019 (registers break/assumption/unknown\nhave no CLI write path) and should probably be resolved with it \u2014 the same\nquestion, one table over.\n\nDELIVERABLE (decide, then make the code and the contract agree)\n- either give the fields a write path (`answer --option-cost` / `--option-breaks`,\n  or the per-option reason form AUG-057 proposes), and document them; OR\n- drop them from the declared table and from `dump`'s rendering, and say in\n  docs/VERBS.md why they were removed\n\nNeither answer is wrong; shipping a column no verb can fill and printing '\u2014' for\nit is.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- `dump` never renders a field that no verb can populate\n- if the fields stay, a user can set them from the CLI and a test proves the value\n  round-trips into `dump`\n- docs/VERBS.md's `option` description matches whichever choice was made\n- the resolution is recorded on AUG-019 so the two register/option gaps close\n  consistently", "capability_tags": ["+decision", "+docs"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
 {"id": "AUG-059", "title": "status contradicts itself on one line: a domain is reported 'answered' while the same line says '(row status NOT-REACHED)'", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 1, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run, namespace df5-real, 3 decisions)\n\n`status` renders each domain of the 44-grid on one line, and for a domain that\ncarries decisions that line says two contradictory things:\n\n  4.09  layout   answered   triage 4  floor 2  terminating ring none (not opened)\n                 decisions/questions 2  (row status NOT-REACHED)\n\n  4.10  build    answered   ...  decisions/questions 1  (row status NOT-REACHED)\n\nThe line leads with \"answered\" and ends with \"(row status NOT-REACHED)\". The two\nlabels are computed from different sources \u2014 the leading word from the presence\nof decision/question rows carrying that domain number, the parenthetical from the\nseeded `domain` row's own `status` column \u2014 and nothing reconciles them.\n\nA reader cannot tell which is true, and the parenthetical is the misleading half:\nafter two decisions in 4.09 the domain is emphatically not \"not reached\". Note the\nseeded rows start NOT-REACHED and no verb appears to advance that column when a\ndecision lands in the domain, so the coverage map \u2014 the single most useful thing\n`status` produces \u2014 under-reports what has been decided.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n- make the domain row's own status advance when a decision/question lands in that\n  domain (the same transition `propagate` performs for its ring logic, if that\n  logic exists), so the parenthetical stops lying; OR\n- if the two are deliberately different axes, label them so a reader can tell \u2014\n  the line must not carry an apparent contradiction\n- either way, a domain with decisions must not render as NOT-REACHED\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- after a decision is recorded in domain 4.NN, `status` never prints\n  \"answered ... (row status NOT-REACHED)\" on that domain's line\n- the seeded NOT-REACHED rows for untouched domains still read NOT-REACHED\n- a test asserts the domain line's two labels agree after a decision lands\n- ruff check auger.py clean", "capability_tags": ["+engine", "+ops"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
 {"id": "AUG-060", "title": "the documented fixed substrate port is unsafe on a shared host: a fresh agent on the CI box got 500s answered by OTHER agents' orphaned daemons", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run's ephemeral-bunker install leg)\n\nThe README's Quick start requires \"a running DuckBrain on 127.0.0.1:3000\" and the\nSubstrate pin section gives the boot line with no --port. On the CI box\n(bunker-las-03, a multi-user rootless-Docker host) that assumption silently\nbreaks, in a way that produces a WRONG ERROR rather than a clear one.\n\nObserved on a fresh agent (d1a530b6), following the docs verbatim:\n  init -> could not list namespaces (500): {'error': \"Cannot determine the\n          duckbrain root: no duckbrain.config.json was found walking up from\n          /home/bunker-864a37ab/duckbrain/src/config, ...\"}\n\n/home/bunker-864a37ab is a DIFFERENT, already-DESTROYED dogfood agent's home\ndirectory. The box was holding four daemons at once: `kara`'s on :3000, and\norphans from destroyed agents 4b004b7a (started 00:48) and 864a37ab (Sep 22)\nstill bound to :3100/:3210/:3311 and still answering requests. Because the fresh\nagent's own boot had silently failed to win a port (or the request simply reached\na stranger's listener), auger reported a 500 whose error text names a path that\nno longer exists \u2014 the worst possible diagnostic.\n\nIsolating on a verified-free port and pinning DUCKBRAIN_URL made the same\ndocumented loop pass in 1 SECOND, so this is purely a port/addressing problem,\nnot an auger or substrate correctness problem.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n- README Substrate pin: give the boot line a non-default port for shared hosts\n  (`node bin/duckbrain.js http --port 39xx`) and say WHY (a fixed 127.0.0.1:3000\n  collides on any multi-user box, and another user's daemon will answer you);\n  add the one-line port-free check (`ss -tlnp | grep <port>` or a connect probe)\n  before starting, and the DUCKBRAIN_URL export that points auger at it\n- diagnostics.md: record the failure signature \u2014 a 500 whose message names a\n  home directory that is not yours means you reached someone else's substrate;\n  the fix is domain separation by port, never killing the other daemon (you may\n  not own it)\n- optionally, auger itself should treat an unexpected 500 at init as this class\n  and say so (a foreign-substrate hint when the error text names a path outside\n  $HOME)\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- the README documents a port-explicit boot line for shared hosts plus the\n  DUCKBRAIN_URL pin, and a fresh agent following ONLY the docs reaches a stored\n  decision (this run needed the undocumented isolation step)\n- docs/dogfood/diagnostics.md carries the foreign-substrate failure signature\n- no change requires touching another user's process, or any repo\n  visibility/permission/clone setting", "capability_tags": ["+ops", "+docs"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
